Q: How can I enumerate an enum? A: <p>I do not hold the opinion this is better, or even good. I am just stating yet another solution.</p>
<p>If enum values range strictly from 0 to n - 1, a generic alternative is:</p>
<pre><code>public void EnumerateEnum&lt;T&gt;()
{
    int length = Enum.GetValues(typeof(T)).Length;
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; length; i++)
    {
        var @enum = (T)(object)i;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If enum values are contiguous and you can provide the first and last element of the enum, then:</p>
<pre><code>public void EnumerateEnum()
{
    for (var i = Suit.Spade; i &lt;= Suit.Diamond; i++)
    {
        var @enum = i;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But that's not strictly enumerating, just looping. The second method is much faster than any other approach though...</p>

Q: Generic Type Info at Runtime (Java) A: <p>You can do <code>obj instanceof Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;</code> when it is known <em>at compile-time</em> that the type argument is correct (which it is in your example, as <code>obj</code> has type <code>Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;</code>). <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/70971904/2711488">This is a newer feature</a>.</p>
<p>However, you still can’t do <code>obj instanceof Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;</code> when the type argument can not be checked, for example when the type of <code>obj</code> is <code>Object</code>. Try</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Object obj = new Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;();
System.out.println(obj instanceof Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;);
</code></pre>
<p>and you’ll get the compiler error as stated by the book, even in newer Java versions.</p>
<hr />
<p>Note that in your example, you are testing for the exact type already known at compile-time, but it will also work when an actual runtime check is involved.<br />
So when the example is changed to test for a subtype (maybe that’s what you originally intended):</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Gen&lt;Integer&gt; obj = new Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;();
System.out.println(obj instanceof Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;);
</code></pre>
<p>it still works, as the compiler knows that when <code>obj</code> is a <code>Gen2</code>, it must be a <code>Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;</code> as only a <code>Gen2&lt;Integer&gt;</code> can be assigned to <code>Gen&lt;Integer&gt;</code> (in an otherwise correct generic program that doesn’t use unsafe cast or raw types).</p>
